{"name":"Figure Stone","hex":"#eedac3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"figure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A drawing or diagram conveying information.","The representation of any form, as by drawing, painting, modelling, carving, embroidering, etc.; especially, a representation of the human body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To calculate, to solve a mathematical problem.","To come to understand."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/figure"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Picture stone","blocks":[{"text":"A picture stone, image stone or figure stone is an ornate slab of stone, usually limestone, which was raised in Germanic Iron Age or Viking Age Scandinavia, and in the greatest number on Gotland. More than four hundred picture stones are known today. All of the stones were probably erected as memorial stones, but only rarely beside graves. Some of them have been positioned where many people could see them at bridges and on roads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Hammars_%28I%29.JPG/330px-Hammars_%28I%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture_stone","wikidata":"Q602202"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ardre_Odin_Sleipnir.jpg/330px-Ardre_Odin_Sleipnir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ardre_Odin_Sleipnir.jpg/330px-Ardre_Odin_Sleipnir.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail of Odin entering Valhalla riding on Sleipnir from the Tjängvide image stone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArdre_Odin_Sleipnir.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Bildstein-Fornsalen_02.jpg/500px-Bildstein-Fornsalen_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Bildstein-Fornsalen_02.jpg/500px-Bildstein-Fornsalen_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image stones on display in Gotland Museum\n. The center bottom stone is known as the Snake-witch stone, featuring a figure holding snakes in each hand.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABildstein-Fornsalen_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Kummakivi_balancing_rock_in_Ruokolahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut